1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,487
A letter arrives.

2
00:00:02,537 --> 00:00:11,741
Your fixed rate is ending, or a friend mentions they pay less.

3
00:00:11,791 --> 00:00:19,949
And you start to wonder: should we switch our mortgage?

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,909
Switching just means moving your mortgage to a new lender, or to a new rate, so that you pay less.

5
00:00:29,959 --> 00:00:35,397
Your home stays exactly the same. Only the loan moves.

6
00:00:35,447 --> 00:00:44,950
Some people move to a new lender. Others simply change rate with the lender they already have.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,257
Here is why people do it.

8
00:00:47,307 --> 00:00:51,226
A lower rate means a lower monthly payment.

9
00:00:51,276 --> 00:01:04,150
On a three hundred thousand euro mortgage with twenty-five years left, one percentage point can be about a hundred and seventy euro a month.

10
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:14,950
That is room for the things that matter: a birthday, a weekend with the grandchildren, time with the people you love.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,759
But switching has costs.

12
00:01:17,809 --> 00:01:25,251
A solicitor. A valuation of your home. A small registration fee.

13
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:32,508
And if you are on a fixed rate, a break fee for leaving early.

14
00:01:32,558 --> 00:01:39,180
Some lenders offer cashback, or help with the legal fees.

15
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:49,950
So add it all up, and compare it with what you would save. Ask for the full cost in writing.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,397
After that, the steps are simple.

17
00:01:53,447 --> 00:01:58,412
One: check your current rate, and any break fee.

18
00:01:58,462 --> 00:02:08,338
Two: apply. The new lender checks your income, your credit record, and what your home is worth.

19
00:02:08,388 --> 00:02:17,009
Three: a solicitor does the legal work, and your new mortgage pays off the old one.

20
00:02:17,059 --> 00:02:24,950
Four: you start on your new rate. It often takes around two or three months.

21
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,978
Before you switch, ask three questions.

22
00:02:29,028 --> 00:02:32,904
Is there a break fee on my fixed rate?

23
00:02:32,954 --> 00:02:36,003
Am I giving up a tracker rate?

24
00:02:36,053 --> 00:02:40,755
And will my mortgage protection stay in place?

25
00:02:40,805 --> 00:02:49,949
If the answers are good, switching can be well worth it. If not, staying put is fine too.

26
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,449
Not sure where you stand?

27
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:59,949
Start with what you want from your mortgage. LifeMap helps you see the way.
